<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Minden</h1><p class="st-meta">1180 CE – 1648 CE (approx.) · capital Petershagen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Weser valley church state founded after Charlemagne's Saxon wars and secularised into a Brandenburg principality in 1648.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minden">Minden</a> was founded around 800 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne's</a> conquest of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saxons">Saxons</a>, on a crossing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> gained counties and immunities from the Ottonian and Salian kings. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> fell in 1180 they ruled a territory along the Weser as imperial princes, though Minden itself grew increasingly self-governing.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The cathedral chapter elected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, who often lived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petershagen">Petershagen</a> because of friction with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minden">Minden's</a> citizens. Their land was small, rural and hemmed in by neighbours such as the counties of Schaumburg, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-ravensberg">Ravensberg</a> and Hoya. The city of Minden adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> in 1530, and in the later 16th century the see was held by Lutheran princes of the houses of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> and Schaumburg.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 the see was secularised as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-minden">Principality of Minden</a> and given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>. It remained Prussian, apart from the Napoleonic period, and the town is now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-minden.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Minden" width="320" height="290"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_Prince-Bishopric_of_Minden_(1560).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1180 CE – 1648 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Petershagen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, then Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hochstift Minden</dd></dl></aside></div>
